  "solution": {
    "text": "Given,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mrow> <mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>{</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>:</mo></mrow> </math> the word <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi>x</mi> </math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"> <mi>y</mi> </math> have atleast one letter in common}<br /><br />Let,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>{</mo><mi>cat</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi>toy</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi>you</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>}</mo></math><br /><br />Clearly, <math><mi>R</mi></math> is reflexive and symmetric but not transitive.<br /><br />[Since, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cat</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>toy</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>toy</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>you</mi><mo mathvariant=\"italic\">⇏</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>cat</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>you</mi></math>]"
  }
